# Day 05 — lab/terraform/.gitignore
#
# Repository-wide Terraform hygiene. Each directory also has its own
# .gitignore; this one is the backstop for anything created outside them.
#
# The auditor (IAC-003, IAC-012) reads .gitignore files from the scanned
# directory upwards, exactly as git resolves them.

# STATE. The single most important line in this file.
#
# A committed .tfstate is a committed list of every resource, subnet ID and
# secret Terraform has ever touched, in plaintext JSON, in your git history
# forever. Rotating the secret is the only remediation; deleting the commit
# is not one, because the object is still in every clone.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Provider caches. A few hundred MB per directory, machine-specific,
# regenerated by `terraform init`. There are four such directories in this
# lab and nothing on earth ever cleans them up — this is Day 05's orphaned
# disk-space trap. Find them with:
#   find . -type d -name .terraform
.terraform/

# Saved plan files. `terraform plan -out=tfplan` writes a binary that can
# contain sensitive values in the clear. CI should treat it as a secret
# artefact with a short retention, never as a build output.
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Real variable values. The .example files ARE committed and are the
# documentation.
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars
!terraform.tfvars.example

# ==========================================================================
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED: .terraform.lock.hcl
# ==========================================================================
#
# The dependency lock file records the exact provider versions selected and
# their checksums for every platform. COMMIT IT. That file is what makes
# `terraform init` produce the same provider binary on your laptop, your
# colleague's laptop and in CI.
#
# Gitignore it and you get the failure mode where the pipeline picks up a
# provider release from three hours ago and proposes a diff nobody wrote.
#
# `terraform init -upgrade` is the only thing that should ever change it, and
# that change belongs in its own reviewed pull request.
#
# Day 04's .gitignore listed this file. That was wrong. IAC-012 is the check
# that catches it.
